WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.440
From a real need to a live, well-timed session.

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.720
This is the video for module two of the Training-of-Trainers course.

3
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:16.140
Weak trainings start with: what do I want to talk about? Strong ones start with: what must people be able to do afterwards?

4
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:29.120
Analysis. Talk to five to ten future participants. And ask a hard question: is training the real solution? Training fixes gaps in knowledge and skill, not missing tools or money.

5
00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:34.530
Design. Write what learners must be able to do, and how you will check.

6
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.170
Development. Make the trainer guide, handouts and test.

7
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.890
Implementation. Deliver the sessions.

8
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.300
Evaluation. Did it work, and what will you improve?

9
00:00:48.550 --> 00:01:00.300
For example, Chioma is planning a half-day training for 25 women who sell cooked food at a motor park in Owerri. Her first aim was: participants will understand food hygiene.

10
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:06.070
But you cannot see someone understand. Words like know or be aware of cannot be observed.

11
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:17.970
So she uses Bloom's action verbs. By the end of the session, participants will be able to demonstrate correct hand washing with soap and running water for at least 20 seconds.

12
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.060
Explain two ways uncovered food gets contaminated. And draw up a three-step routine for her own stall.

13
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:29.830
The formula is: by when, able to, a verb, a task, and a standard.

14
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:42.470
A session plan is the trainer's map: time, activity, what the trainer does, what participants do, and materials. In a ToT, it is your best protection against dilution.

15
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.980
Open with the purpose and objectives in plain words.

16
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.730
Get people involved early with a role play, demonstration or case story.

17
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.730
Let pairs or small groups talk about what happened.

18
00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.650
Give a short input, usually 10 to 15 minutes at a time.

19
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.440
Then practice, with you moving round to help.

20
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.550
Close with a quick check against the objectives, and one action each person will take.

21
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:18.090
Ask of every block: does this help them reach an objective? If not, cut it, even if it is interesting.

22
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:27.920
For example, Fatima has 180 minutes with a cooperative in Kano, but her first plan has 230 minutes of activities.

23
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:36.870
She protects 15 minutes for late arrivals and a 20-minute break. That leaves 145 minutes of teaching time.

24
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.500
A long history of cooperatives and a second icebreaker support no objective, so she cuts both.

25
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:52.110
She cuts her input from 35 to 15 minutes with a printed handout, and lets only two groups report.

26
00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:02.210
Her final plan is 130 minutes, with a 15-minute buffer, and practice time stays intact. That is where most learning happens.

27
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:09.410
Ask open questions starting with what, how or why, and then wait. Count silently to five.

28
00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:16.500
If one woman dominates, thank her, invite someone who has not spoken, and give her a role such as timekeeper.

29
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.150
For a quiet woman, use pairs first so she can rehearse with one person.

30
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.410
An off-topic question goes on a parking lot sheet, and you return to it at the break.

31
00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:42.890
Microteaching is the heart of a ToT. Each trainee teaches a short segment to a small group of peers. For example, in Jos, Rahila teaches a 12-minute segment on filling a savings passbook.

32
00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:58.030
Feedback is specific and balanced. What went well: your objective was clear. Then situation, behaviour, impact: when you checked the sheets, you corrected errors yourself, so learners did not find out why they were wrong.

33
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.870
Rahila re-plans and re-teaches the next day, and this time the learners find their own errors.

34
00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:13.750
The teacher speaks first. Peers give one strength and one improvement each. No one comments on voice, accent or dress.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:29.890
To remember. Check that training is the real solution. Write objectives with verbs you can see. Every activity supports an objective. Protect settling time, breaks and a buffer. And practise with the microteaching cycle.

36
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:38.590
Now run a mini needs analysis with five people, write a two-hour session plan, and run a microteach with feedback.

37
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.880
Continue with the lessons below.
